r.\n\nThe tables are linked through `order_id`, forming a one-to-many relationship.\n\n**Implications for Data Modeling**\n\nDirectly joining raw order and item records may produce multiple rows per order, potentially leading to double-counting in order-level calculations.\n\nTo address this, item-level monetary values will be aggregated by `order_id` before being joined to the order-level dataset.\n\nThis approach supports the construction of a reusable analytical model while preserving the appropriate granularity for sales and customer analyses.\n\nThe next section validates the structural consistency of the source data and establishes monetary KPI definitions.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 2. Data Validation & Metric Definitions\n\nBefore performing business analysis, I examine the\ndistribution of order statuses and their associated\nitem values to establish consistent KPI definitions.\n\n\n- Check duplicate orders and missing item relationships.\n- Validate item counts.\n- Investigate order statuses.\n- Define recorded, fulfilled-status, and cancelled/returned item values.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 2.1 Data Integrity Validation\n\n**Objective:** Verify the structural integrity of the order-level dataset before performing business analysis.\n\nThree validation checks were conducted:\n\n1. **Order uniqueness:** Compare the total number of orders with the number of distinct order IDs.\n2. **Referential integrity:** Identify orders without corresponding records in the `order_items` table.\n3. **Item count consistency:** Compare the recorded `num_of_item` with the actual number of items associated with each order.\n\n**Validation Results**\n\nThe validation query returned zero orders without matching items and zero item-count mismatches. Within each order-status group, the total order count matched the distinct order count.\n\nThese results support the structural consistency of the order-level dataset for subsequent analysis.\n\n*Note: Results reflect the dataset at the time of execution and should be revalidated if the source data changes.*","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"validation_query =  \"\"\"\n\nWITH order_item_totals AS (\n    SELECT order_id, \n        COUNT(*) AS item_count,\n        SUM(sale_price) AS item_value,\n    FROM `bigquery-public-data.thelook_ecommerce.order_items`\n    GROUP BY order_id\n)\n\nSELECT o.status, \n    COUNT(*) as total_orders,\n    SUM(COALESCE(i.item_count,0)) AS total_items,\n    ROUND(SUM(i.item_value),2) AS recorded_item_value,\n    COUNT(DISTINCT o.order_id) AS unique_orders,\n    COUNTIF(i.order_id IS NULL) AS orders_without_items,\n    COUNTIF(o.num_of_item != COALESCE(i.item_count,0)) as item_count_mismatches,\n    COUNTIF(o.num_of_item IS NULL) AS missing_recorded_item_count\n    \nFROM `bigquery-public-data.thelook_ecommerce.orders` as o\n    LEFT JOIN order_item_totals as i\n        ON o.order_id = i.order_id\nWHERE DATE(o.created_at) <= DATE '2026-09-30'\nGROUP BY status\n\"\"\"\ndata_validation = client.query(validation_query).to_dataframe()\ndata_validation\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-09T15:25:02.082611Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-09T15:25:02.082928Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-09T15:25:03.830502Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-09T15:25:02.082895Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-09T15:25:03.829559Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","text":"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages/google/cloud/bigquery/table.py:2086: UserWarning: BigQuery Storage module not found, fetch data with the REST endpoint instead.\n  warnings.warn(\n","output_type":"stream"},{"execution_count":155,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"       status  total_orders  total_items  recorded_item_value  unique_orders  \\\n0   Cancelled         16942        24597           1476098.16          16942   \n1    Complete         28413        41239           2433685.61          28413  