interconnected relational tables, each representing a different level of granularity.\n\n| Source Table | Granularity | Primary Key |\n|---|---|---|\n| `orders` | One record per order | `order_id` |\n| `order_items` | One record per order item | `id` |\n| `products` | One record per product | `id` |\n| `users` | One record per customer | `id` |\n\nThe `orders` and `order_items` tables have a one-to-many relationship, meaning a single order can contain multiple items.\n\n**Target Model: `fact_orders`**\n\nThe primary analytical model will maintain one row per order. To preserve this granularity and avoid duplication of order-level metrics, item-level records will first be aggregated by `order_id` before joining with the `orders` table.\n\nThis model will support consistent calculations of order volume, average order value, customer purchasing behavior, and monthly sales performance.\n\nProduct-level analyses will continue to use item-level records to preserve individual product information.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 3.2 Source Data Preparation\n\n**Objective:** Transform raw transactional data into structured datasets for building a consistent order-level analytical model.\n\nTwo Common Table Expressions (CTEs) are used to prepare the source data.\n\n**1. `orders_in_scope` — Order-Level Data**\n\n- Selects relevant order attributes, including `order_id`, `user_id`, `status`, and `num_of_item`.\n- Restricts the analysis to orders created on or before September 30, 2026.\n- Derives `order_date` and `order_month` to support time-based analysis.\n- Preserves all order statuses for subsequent KPI calculations.\n\n**2. `order_item_totals` — Aggregated Item-Level Data**\n\n- Groups individual order items by `order_id`.\n- Calculates `item_count` using `COUNT(*)`.\n- Calculates `recorded_item_value` using `SUM(sale_price)`.\n- Restricts item records to orders within the defined analysis scope.\n\n**Modeling Rationale**\n\nSince one order can contain multiple items, directly joining the raw `orders` and `order_items` tables produces multiple rows per order.\n\nTo maintain the intended order-level grain, item-level transactions are aggregated by `order_id` before being joined to the order records.\n\nThis prevents duplication of order-level observations and provides a consistent foundation for subsequent sales and customer analyses.\n\n**Outcome**\n\nThe prepared datasets are used to construct the `fact_orders` analytical model in the following section.\n\n*Note: The source data is synthetic and may change between executions. Validation results are documented separately to avoid duplicating data-quality findings.*","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"data_prep_query = \"\"\"\n                    WITH orders_in_scope AS (\n                        SELECT\n                            order_id,\n                            user_id,\n                            created_at,\n                            DATE(created_at) AS order_date,\n                            DATE_TRUNC(DATE(created_at), MONTH) AS order_month,\n                            status,\n                            num_of_item\n                        FROM `bigquery-public-data.thelook_ecommerce.orders`\n                        WHERE DATE(created_at) <= DATE '2026-09-30'\n                    ),\n\n                    order_item_totals AS (\n                        SELECT\n                            oi.order_id,\n                            COUNT(*) AS item_count,\n                            SUM(oi.sale_price) AS recorded_item_value\n                        FROM `bigquery-public-data.thelook_ecommerce.order_items` as oi\n                        INNER JOIN orders_in_scope as o\n                            ON oi.order_id = o.order_id\n                        GROUP BY oi.order_id\n                    )\n\n                    SELECT\n                        (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders_in_scope)\n                            AS total_orders,\n                    \n                        (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM order_item_totals)\n                            AS orders_with_items,\n                    \n                        (SELECT SUM(item_count) FROM order_item_totals)\n                            AS total_items,\n                    \n                        (SELECT ROUND(SUM(recorded_item_value), 2)\n                         FROM order_item_totals)\n                            AS recorded_item_value\n                    \"\"\"\n\ndata_prep = client.query(data_prep_query).to_dataframe()\ndata_prep\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-09T15:25:04.873628Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-09T15:25:04.873965Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-09T15:25:05.904619Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-09T15:25:04.873932Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-09T15:25:05.903818Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","text":"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages/google/cloud/bigquery/table.py:2086: UserWarning: BigQuery Storage module not found, fetch data with the REST endpoint instead.\n  warnings.warn(\n","output_type":"stream"},{"execution_count":158,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"   total_orders  orders_with_items  total_items  recorded_item_value\n0        112979             112979       164261            9812458.8","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>total_orders</th>\n      <th>orders_with_items</th>\n      <th>total_items</th>\n      <th>recorded_item_value</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>112979</td>\n      <td>112979</td>\n      <td>164261</td>\n      <td>9812458.8</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":158},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 3.3 Building a Reusable Order-Level Analytical Model\n\n**Objective:** Develop a reusable SQL model that consolidates order-level attributes and item-level monetary values while preserving one row per order.\n\nThe model consists of three CTEs:\n\n1. **`orders_in_scope`** — Selects orders within the reporting period and derives date-based attributes.\n2. **`order_item_totals`** — Aggregates item-level transactions to the order level.\n3. **`fact_orders`** — Combines order attributes with aggregated item values and calculates status-specific monetary metrics.\n\n**Model Grain:** One row per `order_id`.\n\n**Key Metrics:**\n- `recorded_item_value` — Item value across all order statuses.\n- `fulfilled_order_value` — Item value associated with Complete and Shipped orders.\n- `cancelled_returned_value` — Item value associated with Cancelled and Returned orders.\n- `processing_order_value` — Item value associated with Processing orders.\n\n**Reusability**\n\nThe CTE definitions are stored in the Python variable `fact_orders_ctes` and reused through the `run_fact_orders_query()` helper function.\n\nThis centralizes KPI logic and reduces duplicated SQL across downstream analyses.\n\n*Note: This approach does not create a persistent BigQuery table or view. The model is evaluated when each analytical query is executed.*","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"fact_orders_ctes = \"\"\"\nWITH orders_in_scope AS (\n    SELECT\n        order_id,\n        user_id,\n        created_at,\n        DATE(created_at) AS order_date,\n        DATE_TRUNC(DATE(created_at), MONTH) AS order_month,\n        status,\n        num_of_item\n    FROM `bigquery-public-data.thelook_ecommerce.orders`\n    WHERE DATE(created_at) <= DATE '2026-09-30'\n),\n\norder_item_totals AS (\n    SELECT\n        oi.order_id,\n        COUNT(*) AS item_count,\n        SUM(oi.sale_price) AS recorded_item_value\n    FROM `bigquery-public-data.thelook_ecommerce.order_items` oi\n    INNER JOIN orders_in_scope o\n        ON oi.order_id = o.order_id\n    GROUP BY oi.order_id\n),\n\nfact_orders AS (\n    SELECT\n        o.order_id,\n        o.user_id,\n        o.created_at,\n        o.order_date,\n        o.order_month,\n        o.status,\n        o.num_of_item,\n        i.item_count,\n\n        -- Value across all order statuses\n        i.recorded_item_value,\n\n        -- Complete and Shipped orders\n        CASE\n            WHEN o.status IN ('Complete', 'Shipped')\n            THEN i.recorded_item_value\n        WHEN i.order_id IS NULL THEN NULL\n        ELSE 0\n    END AS fulfilled_order_value,\n\n    -- Cancelled and Returned orders\n    CASE\n        WHEN o.status IN ('Cancelled', 'Returned')\n        THEN i.recorded_item_value\n        WHEN i.order_id IS NULL THEN NULL\n        ELSE 0\n    END AS cancelled_returned_value,\n\n    -- Processing orders\n    CASE\n        WHEN o.status = 'Processing'\n        THEN i.recorded_item_value\n        WHEN i.order_id IS NULL THEN NULL\n        ELSE 0\n    END AS processing_order_value\n\nFROM orders_in_scope o\nLEFT JOIN order_item_totals i\n    ON o.order_id = i.order_id\n)\n\"\"\"\n\ndef run_fact_orders_query(analysis_sql):\n    full_query = fact_orders_ctes + \"\\n\" + analysis_sql\n    return client.query(full_query).to_dataframe()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-09T15:25:05.905655Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-09T15:25:05.905901Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-09T15:25:05.911940Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-09T15:25:05.905868Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-09T15:25:05.911190Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":159},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Preview the reusable order-level model\n\nfact_orders_preview = run_fact_orders_query(\"\"\"\nSELECT *\nFROM fact_orders\nORDER BY order_id\nLIMIT 10\n\"\"\")\n\nfact_orders_preview","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"