atively similar across most categories.**\n\nMost categories recorded returned-status item shares near **10%**, without substantial differentiation across major categories.\n\nGiven the synthetic nature of the dataset, these differences do not provide sufficient evidence of meaningful category-specific return behavior.\n\n**Analytical Takeaway**\n\nThe analysis demonstrates how conditional aggregation and window functions can quantify status-related monetary exposure at different levels of aggregation.\n\nHowever, the resulting figures should not be interpreted as actual return rates, confirmed refunds, or realized financial losses.\n\n*Limitations: Order statuses are measured at query time, and the dataset does not establish which individual items were physically returned or how much money was refunded.*","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 4.3 Customer Behavior & Segmentation\n\n### 4.3.1 Customer-Level Data Preparation\n\n**Objective:** Transform the validated order-level dataset into a\ncustomer-level summary to analyze purchasing frequency and monetary value.\n\n**Data Grain:** One row per customer (`user_id`).\n\n**Key Metrics:**\n- Fulfilled Order Count: Number of orders with Complete or Shipped status.\n- Total Fulfilled Value: Sum of fulfilled-status item value per customer.\n- First Fulfilled Date: Earliest observed fulfilled-status order date.\n\nOnly customers with at least one fulfilled-status order are included.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\ncustomer_summary_sql = \"\"\"\nSELECT\n    user_id,\n    COUNT(*) AS fulfilled_order_count,\n    SUM(fulfilled_order_value) AS total_fulfilled_value,\n    MIN(order_date) AS first_fulfilled_date\nFROM fact_orders\nWHERE status IN ('Complete', 'Shipped')\n  AND user_id IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY user_id\nORDER BY fulfilled_order_count DESC\n\"\"\"\n\ncustomer_summary = run_fact_orders_query(customer_summary_sql)\n\ncustomer_summary.head(10)\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iop