class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th>month_index</th>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <th>6</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>cohort_month</th>\n      <th></th>\n      <th></th>\n      <th></th>\n      <th></th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2025-01-01</th>\n      <td>780</td>\n      <td>8</td>\n      <td>13</td>\n      <td>7</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2025-02-01</th>\n      <td>817</td>\n      <td>15</td>\n      <td>15</td>\n      <td>12</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2025-03-01</th>\n      <td>871</td>\n      <td>12</td>\n      <td>13</td>\n      <td>15</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2025-04-01</th>\n      <td>827</td>\n      <td>10</td>\n      <td>11</td>\n      <td>14</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2025-05-01</th>\n      <td>915</td>\n      <td>12</td>\n      <td>13</td>\n      <td>22</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":184},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\noverall_retention = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"month_index\": [1, 3, 6],\n\n    \"active_customers\": [\n        counts_matrix[m].sum()\n        for m in [1, 3, 6]\n    ],\n\n    \"cohort_size\": counts_matrix[0].sum()\n})\n\n# Calculate weighted retention percentage\noverall_retention[\"retention_pct\"] = (\n    overall_retention[\"active_customers\"]\n    / overall_retention[\"cohort_size\"]\n    * 100\n)\n\ndisplay(overall_retention.round(2))\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-09T15:25:26.609275Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-09T15:25:26.609772Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-09T15:25:26.634109Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-09T15:25:26.609743Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-09T15:25:26.633314Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"   month_index  active_customers  cohort_size  retention_pct\n0            1               167        11383           1.47\n1            3               199        11383           1.75\n2            6               212        11383           1.86","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>month_index</th>\n      <th>active_customers</th>\n      <th>cohort_size</th>\n      <th>retention_pct</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>167</td>\n      <td>11383</td>\n      <td>1.47</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>199</td>\n      <td>11383</td>\n      <td>1.75</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>6</td>\n      <td>212</td>\n      <td>11383</td>\n      <td>1.86</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":185},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Key Findings — Customer Cohort Analysis\n\n**1. Low observed monthly repeat purchasing activity**\n\nAcross the 2025 first-observed fulfilled-purchase cohorts (11,383 customers), cohort-size-weighted purchasing retention was:\n\n- **M+1:** 1.47% (167 active customers)\n- **M+3:** 1.75% (199 active customers)\n- **M+6:** 1.86% (212 active customers)\n\nThese results suggest that relatively few customers made another fulfilled-status purchase in each measured month.\n\n**2. Monthly purchasing retention does not necessarily decline over time**\n\nThe M+6 purchasing retention rate was slightly higher than M+1. However, this does not imply improving customer loyalty. Customers are independently counted as active in each month, even if they did not purchase in preceding months.\n\n**3. Cohort-level variation**\n\nThe retention heatmap shows variation across the 2025 cohorts, with most observed monthly purchasing retention rates around 1%–2%. These fluctuations are descriptive and do not establish meaningful behavioral differences between cohorts.\n\n**Methodology and Limitations**\n\n- Cohorts are defined by each customer's first observed fulfilled-status order.\n- Active customers are counted once per calendar month.\n- Overall retention is weighted by cohort size rather than calculated as a simple average of cohort retention rates.\n- All 2025 cohorts have a complete M+6 observation window through September 2026.\n- TheLook is a synthetic dataset; observed patterns should not be interpreted as evidence of real-world customer behavior.\n- Fulfilled-status purchases are operational proxies and do not necessarily represent recognized revenue.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 5. Executive Summary & Business Implications\n\n### 5.1 Project Overview\n\nThis project uses **Google BigQuery SQL and Python** to analyze TheLook's synthetic e-commerce dataset, focusing on sales performance, category contribution, customer purchasing behavior, and monthly cohort retention.\n\nA reusable order-level analytical model was developed to ensure consistent KPI definitions and avoid double counting. Data reconciliation checks were performed across different levels of aggregation.\n\n### 5.2 Key Findings\n\n**1. Sales growth was primarily associated with higher order volume.**\n\nBetween January and September 2026, fulfilled-status item value reached **$1.59M**, representing **73.43% year-over-year growth** compared with the same period in 2025.\n\nFulfilled orders increased from **10,729 to 18,621 (+73.56%)**, while average order value remained nearly unchanged, declining by **0.07%**.\n\nThe results indicate that the increase in fulfilled-status monetary value was primarily associated with higher order volume rather than larger individual orders.\n\n**2. Product categories differ in sales contribution and estimated profitability.**\n\n**Outerwear & Coats** was the leading category, generating **$672.9K** in fulfilled-status item value (12.51% of the total) and approximately **$373.6K** in estimated gross profit.\n\n**Jeans** ranked second in fulfilled-status item value at **$632.7K** (11.76%), despite a higher number of items sold.\n\nThis comparison highlights the importance of examining sales volume, average selling price, estimated gross profit, and gross margin together when evaluating category performance.\n\n**3. Repeat customers contribute disproportionately to fulfilled-status monetary value.**\n\nAmong **48,357 purchasing customers**, repeat customers represented **22.98%** but contributed **40.38%** of fulfilled-status item value.\n\nAverage order values were similar between one-time and repeat customers (**$86.12 vs. $87.00**). The higher monetary contribution per repeat customer was primarily associated with greater purchase frequency.\n\n**4. Monthly cohort retention shows limited observed repeat purchasing activity.**\n\nAcross **11,383 customers** in the 2025 first-observed fulfilled-purchase cohorts, cohort-size-weighted purchasing retention was:\n\n- **M+1:** 1.47%\n- **M+3:** 1.75%\n- **M+6:** 1.86%\n\nThese percentages measure purchasing activity within specific calendar months, not continuous customer retention. Changes between monthly rates should not be interpreted as evidence of improving customer loyalty.\n\n### 5.3 Business Implications\n\nThe analysis demonstrates how an e-commerce business could use integrated transactional metrics to support performance monitoring.\n\n- **Sales monitoring:** Evaluate monetary growth alongside order volume and average order value to identify its main components.\n- **Category evaluation:** Compare sales contribution and estimated gross profitability instead of relying on item sales or revenue share alone.\n- **Customer engagement:** Monitor purchasing frequency and repeat customer contribution alongside average order value.\n- **Cohort tracking:** Use cohort-size-weighted monthly purchasing rates to compare customer activity across first-purchase periods.\n\nThese implications describe potential applications of the analytical framework rather than validated interventions.\n\n### 5.4 Technical Skills Demonstrated\n\n- **SQL:** Common Table Expressions (CTEs), joins, conditional aggregation, date functions, window functions, and customer segmentation.\n- **Data Modeling:** Reusable order-level analytical model with clearly defined data grain.\n- **Data Quality:** Structural validation and KPI reconciliation across different aggregation levels.\n- **Python:** Pandas-based data transformation, cohort retention matrices, and Matplotlib visualizations.\n- **Business Analytics:** YoY/YTD analysis, estimated category profitability, customer purchasing frequency, and cohort retention.\n\n### 5.5 Limitations\n\nTheLook is a **synthetic dataset**, so its patterns may not reflect real consumer behavior.\n\nFulfilled-status item value is an operational proxy rather than audited recognized revenue. Estimated gross profit uses available product costs and excludes additional expenses. Historical order statuses are observed at query time rather than reconstructed as of the reporting cutoff.\n\nCustomer cohorts are based on first *observed* fulfilled-status purchases, which may not represent true acquisition dates. Consequently, the findings are descriptive and should not be used to infer causal relationships or confirmed business performance improvements.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null}]}
